/// mlrepro — a generic recursive TRANSFORMER that THREADS A CLOSURE through its recursion declines at ≥2
/// element types (the next iterator unblock after Part C). (iterators stress test, 2026-07-16)
///
/// STATUS: root-caused by v-inference (lower.rs type_specialize ~10318-10340 — the specializer solves a
/// bare-closure argument IN ISOLATION via solved_lambda_arrow from the closure's own body, rather than
/// against the callee's INSTANTIATED parameter type at this call; an identity closure `fn (s) => s` then
/// leaves `s` = Any → declines). Fix in progress; this repro is kept so it becomes a passing regression the
/// tick the fix lands.
///
/// RELATION TO PART C. Part C (7793d4841) tied a recursive-generic PRODUCER's result element to its
/// argument, unblocking from-list + closure-free consumers/transformers (giter.cdz). THIS is the closure-
/// carrying analogue: a recursive-generic transformer that threads a CLOSURE. Precisely isolated:
///   - count/collect (recursive-generic, NO closure) at 2 types → FINE (Part C).
///   - idt(it) = … Cons(h, idt(rest)) (recursive-generic transformer, rebuilds GIter(a), NO closure) at 2
///     types → FINE. So NOT "any recursive-generic transformer".
///   - map(it, f) / filter(it, p) (THREAD the closure f/p through the recursion) at 2 types → CDZ0201.
///   - map at ONE element type → compiles AND runs. So each instantiation is fine alone; the second fails to
///     monomorphize — the Part C symptom, for a closure-threading transformer.
///   - CHECK vs LOWER split (same as Part C): `cdz check` PASSES (exit 0); the decline is at LOWERING.
///
/// EXPECTED: map/filter (a recursive-generic transformer threading a closure) monomorphizes independently
/// per element type, like count/collect/idt already do — opening the whole generic transformer family
/// (map/filter/take-while/scan).

type GIter =
  | Nil
  | Cons(a, GIter(a))

def from-list(xs) = match xs with
  | [] => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | [h, .. t] => GIter.Cons(h, from-list(t))

def count(it) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => 0
  | GIter.Cons(_, rest) => 1 + count(rest)

/// The transformer that threads a closure `f` through its recursion — the shape that declines at ≥2 types.
def map(it, f) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => GIter.Cons(f(h), map(rest, f))

/// FACE 1: `map` over Int64 (a -> a). Compiles + runs ALONE.
@test
def map-over-int() =
  if count(map(from-list([1, 2, 3]), fn(x) => x + 1)) == 3 then unit else trap("map int")

/// FACE 2: `map` over String, SAME PROGRAM. The second instantiation triggers CDZ0201 — the closure-
/// threading transformer fails to monomorphize at a second element type.
@test
def map-over-string() =
  if count(map(from-list(["a", "b"]), fn(s) => s)) == 2 then unit else trap("map str")
